Overview
--------
This dataset was created within the project “Advanced approaches to measuring and processing fMRI data with regard to benefits in neurological research and diagnostics".

Participants underwent two fMRI scanning sessions, approximately 3-5 weeks apart. Data collection took place at two locations: the Multimodal and Functional Imaging Laboratory (MAFIL) at CEITEC Masaryk University in Brno and the Department of Radiology at the University Hospital Olomouc. A total of four MRI scanners were used: two 3T Siemens Magnetom Prisma scanners located at MAFIL (scanner A and scanner B), one 3T Siemens Magnetom Cima.X Fit scanner located at MAFIL (scanner C), and one 3T Siemens Vida scanner located at the University Hospital Olomouc (scanner O).

Participants were divided into groups based on the order and location of scanning sessions. Subjects that were scanned only in Brno resulted in combinations such as AB, BA, BB, CA, AC, and CC, depending on the order of scanners across visits. Participants scanned at different locations, resulting in combinations BO and OB.

During each visit, participants performed three fMRI tasks (a verbal fluency task, a sequential finger opposition task, and a visual oddball task), along with structural scans, field maps, and physiological recordings. Each task was acquired twice using two different acquisition sequences: single-echo (SE) and multi-echo (ME). To minimise potential order effects, the order of task presentation was randomised across participants, yielding six possible randomisations. 


Details about the tasks and measurement
---------------------------------------
The sequential finger tapping task (SFO) consisted of alternating baseline and active blocks. During the baseline block, participants were instructed to keep their eyes closed and remain still without performing any movements. During the active blocks, participants sequentially touched the thumb of their right hand with the other fingers in the following order: index finger, middle finger, ring finger, and little finger. The tapping pace was recommended to be approximately one touch per second. The transitions between baseline and active blocks were indicated by auditory instructions ("Start" and "Stop") delivered through headphones. Each block lasted approximately 15 seconds. Total duration of the task was 4:25 min.

The verbal fluency task (VFT) consisted of alternating baseline and word generation blocks. During the baseline block, participants kept their eyes closed and were instructed to relax without focusing on any specific thoughts. During the active blocks, participants were presented with a letter cue delivered via headphones (e.g., "P as in Petr") and were asked to silently generate as many words as possible beginning with the given letter. Each active block started with the presentation of letter cue and ended with an auditory "Stop" instruction. Baseline and active blocks alternated approximately every 30 seconds. The total duration of the task was 5:26 min.

Three types of stimuli were used in the oddball task (VOB). The first type consists of frequent stimuli (FREQUENT), which make up the majority of all presented items. These are always represented by the letter "O" and require no response from the participant. The second type includes rare target stimuli (TARGET) represented by the liter "X" and participants were instructed to press a button they were holding as quickly and accurately as possible. The third type consists of rare distractor stimuli (DISTRACTOR) represented by other letters of the alphabet. These were included to increase task complexity and no response was required for them. Each stimulus was presented for 500 milliseconds, with an inter-stimulus interval randomly varying between 1.5 and 4 seconds. The total duration of the task was 10:32 min. The order of stimuli in each oddball task was randomized into one of five predefined versions.

During all fMRI tasks, physiological signals were recorded simultaneously, including electrocardiography and respiratory aktivity,  to enable retrospective correction of physiological artifacts in the fMRI signal.At the MAFIL laboratory, physiological signals were acquired using the BrainAmp MR system in combination with Brain Vision Recorder software (Brain Products GmbH, Germany). In Olomouc, physiological signals were recorded using Siemens ECG/Respiratory Unit (PERU_098, Siemens Healthcare GmbH, Germany), with MR-compatible sensors. The recording were synchronised with the fMRI acquisition using the CMRR multiband EPI sequence with BioMatrix technology.

All scanners were equipped with a 64-channel head/neck coil. The visual stimulation setup differed slightly between scanners. On scanner A, participants viewed stimuli on a 24" LCD BOLDscreen a via mirror mounted on the head coil. On scanner B, scanner C and scanner O, visual stimuli were presented using MR-compatible goggles. All functional runs were acquired using both SE and ME EPI sequences. For SE acquisition, the repetition time (TR) was 1020 ms, echo time (TE) 30 ms, flip angle (FA) 50°, and multiband factor (MBF) was 4. For ME acquisition, the repetition time (TR) was 700 ms, with three echo times (TEs) of 14/34.13/54.26 ms, flip angle (FA) 45°, and multiband factor (MBF) was 6. 


Data completeness and limitations
---------------------------------
Most datasets (188/211 participants) follow the standard acquisition protocol. The remaining  participants contain deviations such as missing T1-weighted images, physiological recordings, or individual sessions. In a few cases, logs are incomplete due to technical issues. For one participant, the log for VOB task is missing and log from a participant with the same randomisation was used. However, button-press and reaction-time data are not available for this case. For some participants, two field maps are available due to interrupted measurements. One participant has identical randomization across both sessions. Physiological data quality is generally lower for scanner O. Further details are provided in participants.tsv and data_issues.txt.



